  m_Name: TurbochargerAirIntakeData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Turbocharger Air Intake
  engineDescription: A Turbocharger Air Intake System is an air-handling system that
    supplies compressed air to an internal combustion engine by using the energy
    of exhaust gases to drive a turbocharger compressor. Its main components include
    the air filter, intake duct, compressor wheel, compressor housing, shaft, bearings,
    intercooler, throttle or intake control components, and intake manifold. During
    operation, atmospheric air first passes through the air filter and enters the
    compressor side of the turbocharger. Exhaust gases from the engine rotate the
    turbine, which is connected to the compressor through a common shaft. The rotating
    compressor draws in and compresses the intake air, increasing its pressure and
    density. The compressed air may then pass through an intercooler before entering
    the engine cylinders, allowing a greater mass of air to be supplied for combustion.
    Turbocharged air-intake systems are widely used in automotive engines, diesel
    engines, commercial vehicles, marine engines, and high-performance power systems.
    From an engineering perspective, understanding compressor operation, boost pressure,
    air density, pressure losses, turbocharger matching, intercooling, thermal management,
    filtration, lubrication, and compressor surge is essential for improving engine
    power, fuel efficiency, and reliability.
  engineCategory: Engineering
